WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.235
This is Slot, a booking application for a sports coach.

00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:06.609
I’m opening an isolated client demo.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:11.751
The people are fictional, but reservations use the real booking service and database.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.617
I choose an available session.

00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:20.322
The summary shows the date, time, duration and price, with the calendar’s timezone visible.

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:23.581
The client can check the details before reserving.

00:00:24.213 --> 00:00:27.174
The reservation starts as a ten-minute hold.

00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:31.825
I confirm the clearly labelled demo payment, without charging a card.

00:00:31.825 --> 00:00:33.940
The booking is now confirmed.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.900
Abandoned holds expire and release their place.

00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:42.265
In My sessions, I move the booking to another available time.

00:00:42.265 --> 00:00:44.802
After confirmation, the new time appears.

00:00:44.802 --> 00:00:47.761
The server checks availability again when rescheduling.

00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:52.701
Cancelling asks for confirmation, then displays the cancelled status.

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.518
The place is released.

00:00:54.518 --> 00:00:56.335
These states control availability;

00:00:56.335 --> 00:00:59.060
they are not just decorative labels.

00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:03.966
Switching to the coach reveals the timetable and booking management.

00:01:03.966 --> 00:01:06.874
This role switch belongs to the demo.

00:01:06.874 --> 00:01:10.196
The server still checks permissions for protected operations.

00:01:10.913 --> 00:01:14.644
Finally, two clients request the last place simultaneously.

00:01:14.644 --> 00:01:18.375
One succeeds and the other is safely refused.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:21.173
This exercises the real booking service.

00:01:21.173 --> 00:01:25.371
This recording uses the local application and simulated checkout.
